  "instructions": "# state-machine — plan the state machine of ONE collection or object\n\nPlan the state machine for the node identified by `targetNodeId` in the supplied `scene`, using the sketch image, ordered `compositionStrokes` (including interaction annotations), the node's semantic transformations and the user instruction. Infer meaningful stages of movement, growth, interaction or transformation from the dynamic intent. Never invent motion the sketch/instruction does not suggest; if the node is truly static, return a single state with no transitions.\n\n- States change only THIS node's property values (`values[].propertyId` must be a property id of the target node). Keep each value's structure identical to the base property value: same scalar type and range shape, or the same algorithm with the same argument ids — change only the scalar numbers/colors/options/toggles.\n- If the existing properties cannot express a needed phase difference, still keep the structure; describe the difference in the state description so the code step can branch on the state id.\n- If `scene` already contains a machine for the node, refine it into finer meaningful phases while preserving its intent and stable ids where they still apply.\n- `algorithms` must be `[]` unless you truly need a new definition (then list only new ones).\n\n## PER-NODE STATE MACHINE\n\nA state machine belongs to **one node** (a collection or an object), not to the whole frame. It changes that node's own property values over time or in response to user input. Other nodes keep their own machines.\n\n- `machine = { initialState, states[], transitions[] }`.\n- Each state: `{ id, label, description, values[] }`. `values` lists `{ propertyId, value }` for properties of THIS node whose value differs in that state. Omitted properties keep the node's base value. A state value MUST keep the same structure as the base property value: a number stays a number with the same range shape, an algorithm call stays a call of the same algorithm with the same argument ids — only scalar values change between states. If a phase needs a new controllable quantity, add it as a property of the node first (in `transformation`).\n- The description explains the entry appearance, the behavior while active, the eligible user inputs and the exit/completion condition.\n- Transitions: `{ id, from, to, label, trigger, afterMs }`. `trigger: \"after\"` for genuinely timed phases (afterMs = integer milliseconds 100..60000, long enough to see the motion); `trigger: \"event\"` for real user input or a code-detected completion (afterMs = 1000, ignored). Waiting/hover/pressed states must not advance just because time passed.\n\nFINE-GRAINED STATE PLANNING: Decompose the node's behavior as finely as its actual behavior supports, up to 32 states and 128 transitions. Do not summarize a multi-step dynamic or interactive behavior as only Idle/Active/Done. For a rich interaction normally use 8–20 meaningful states. Separate every change in user intent, input availability, movement direction, speed regime, contact, selection, appearance, growth, completion, cancellation, recovery and return. A drag example can distinguish available, hover feedback, pressed/armed, drag beginning, dragging, valid drop preview, released, snapping/settling, committed and returning; a motion example can distinguish anticipation, acceleration, cruising, approach, contact, response, deceleration, settling and recovery. These are examples, not a template. Do not add duplicate sampled frames of unchanged behavior. A truly static node has no machine.\n\nSEMANTIC TRANSITION LABELS: Every `label` concisely describes WHY the node changes, naming the user action, target or completion condition, e.g. `\"원 위에 포인터 진입\"`, `\"드래그 시작\"`, `\"유효한 영역에 놓기\"`, `\"성장 완료\"`, `\"정착 후 대기\"`. Never use bare IDs, `A→B`, `Next`, `Transition 1` or only a duration. Use stable descriptive ids. Include relevant cancellation, re-entry and return paths. Prefer Korean labels and descriptions. Never render this planning text in the artwork.\n\n## Output JSON\n\nReturn exactly one object matching `prompts/schemas/state-machine.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"algorithms\": [],\n  \"machine\": {\n    \"initialState\": \"idle\",\n    \"states\": [\n      { \"id\": \"idle\", \"label\": \"대기\", \"description\": \"원이 작게 숨 쉬며 클릭을 기다립니다.\", \"values\": [ { \"propertyId\": \"radius\", \"value\": { \"type\": \"number\", \"value\": 24, \"min\": 10, \"max\": 140, \"step\": 1, \"unit\": \"px\" } } ] },\n      { \"id\": \"growing\", \"label\": \"성장\", \"description\": \"클릭 후 1.2초 동안 커집니다.\", \"values\": [ { \"propertyId\": \"radius\", \"value\": { \"type\": \"number\", \"value\": 90, \"min\": 10, \"max\": 140, \"step\": 1, \"unit\": \"px\" } } ] },\n      { \"id\": \"settled\", \"label\": \"정착\", \"description\": \"커진 상태로 머무르고 다시 클릭하면 대기로 돌아갑니다.\", \"values\": [] }\n    ],\n    \"transitions\": [\n      { \"id\": \"press-circle\", \"from\": \"idle\", \"to\": \"growing\", \"label\": \"원 클릭\", \"trigger\": \"event\", \"afterMs\": 1000 },\n      { \"id\": \"growth-done\", \"from\": \"growing\", \"to\": \"settled\", \"label\": \"성장 완료\", \"trigger\": \"after\", \"afterMs\": 1200 },\n      { \"id\": \"press-reset\", \"from\": \"settled\", \"to\": \"idle\", \"label\": \"다시 클릭해 되돌리기\", \"trigger\": \"event\", \"afterMs\": 1000 }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```",
